1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,875
Hi, I'm Tendry Tsilavina Razanamalala.

2
00:00:03,125 --> 00:00:07,812
I'm a full-stack developer from Madagascar and the founder of Tirex Agency.

3
00:00:08,062 --> 00:00:13,875
I build web, mobile and AI software, and I like tools that solve problems people really have.

4
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:18,062
For the Meta Global AI Developer Hackathon, I built RecupBuild.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:22,162
A lot of people keep piles of reclaimed material:

6
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:26,475
old planks, plywood, sheet metal, leftovers from a building site.

7
00:00:26,725 --> 00:00:29,475
The wood is there. What's missing is a plan.

8
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:33,100
Plans online assume you buy new boards at exact sizes.

9
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:37,475
Measuring every piece by hand and working out the cuts takes time,

10
00:00:37,725 --> 00:00:40,975
and one mistake wastes the little material you have.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,938
RecupBuild turns this around: you start from your pieces, not from a catalogue.

12
00:00:47,188 --> 00:00:50,688
You put an A4 sheet next to the pile and take one photo.

13
00:00:50,938 --> 00:00:55,125
Meta's Muse Spark model reads the photo, uses the sheet as a ruler,

14
00:00:55,375 --> 00:00:57,562
and finds and measures every piece.

15
00:00:57,812 --> 00:01:00,875
Then it suggests projects that fit your material.

16
00:01:01,125 --> 00:01:05,938
A cutting engine places every part on your real planks, counting the saw cut.

17
00:01:06,188 --> 00:01:08,375
The AI proposes; the engine checks.

18
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,212
Let's run it on the real app.

19
00:01:11,462 --> 00:01:15,900
I start with a real photo of my offcuts, with an A4 sheet next to them.

20
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:19,150
The analysis takes a moment, so I've sped it up.

21
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:24,650
Here's the inventory: every piece is outlined and numbered on the photo,

22
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,838
with its length, width and estimated thickness.

23
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:30,150
If something's off, I can fix it.

24
00:01:34,924 --> 00:01:38,112
Next, the projects, each one sized to these pieces.

25
00:01:38,362 --> 00:01:39,049
I pick one.

26
00:01:48,291 --> 00:01:51,416
And here's the plan: a 3D model I can turn around,

27
00:01:51,666 --> 00:01:53,666
the cuts drawn on my own planks,

28
00:01:53,916 --> 00:01:57,541
the list of parts, and the assembly steps, ready to print.

29
00:02:08,191 --> 00:02:10,128
There's a second mode: Rebuild.

30
00:02:10,378 --> 00:02:12,628
I give it a photo of a table I like.

31
00:02:12,878 --> 00:02:16,503
It breaks it down into the parts to cut, the tools I need,

32
00:02:16,753 --> 00:02:18,816
and the order to put it together.

33
00:02:30,269 --> 00:02:34,957
It's for makers, small workshops, schools and communities on tight budgets:

34
00:02:35,207 --> 00:02:39,707
reclaimed material becomes shelves, storage and furniture, from a phone.

35
00:02:39,957 --> 00:02:42,707
Next, I want to test it with real workshops,

36
00:02:42,957 --> 00:02:46,207
support more materials and joints, and add Malagasy.

37
00:02:47,169 --> 00:02:49,982
RecupBuild: build with what you already have.

38
00:02:50,232 --> 00:02:52,232
I'm Tendry. Thanks for watching.

